  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nGlobal model guidance remains in agreement that the large-scale \nupper-level wave pattern across North America will amplify over the \nweekend as the aforementioned amplifying ridge migrates eastward and \na trough deepens over the West Coast. Most of the forecast interest \nand uncertainty, however, is focused on the evolution of Tropical \nDepression Nine, currently in the southwestern Gulf of Mexico. This \ntropical cyclone will likely continue strengthening into a hurricane \nand make landfall somewhere along the northern Gulf Coast as it is \nsteered northward between the West Coast trough and a subtropical \nupper-level ridge/high across the eastern Atlantic Ocean as well as \ninteraction with an upper-level low initially over the Southern \nPlains. After landfall, most ensemble and deterministic global and \ntropical-cyclone models track the weakening tropical cyclone to the \nsouth and southeast of the Mid-Mississippi River Valley late \nSaturday through Sunday with just some increasing upper-level \nclouds, but the northwestward-most members have tracks into \nsoutheastern MO and southern IL which would include showers and more \nabundant cloud cover reaching the CWA. The track and timing of this \ntropical system is very uncertain and it may not be until much much \ncloser to that time that resolution improves more considerably. \nAlthough there is strong support for continued above average \ntemperatures through the weekend into early next week, the NBM \ninterquartile range expands to around 6 to 8 F since it is not clear \nif temperatures will be cooled by clouds and precipitation.",
